== Why Are We Saved By Faith?

#smallcaps[Why] is Faith chosen as the channel of Salvation? No doubt this question is often asked. “By grace you#super[+] have been saved #emph[through faith],” is certainly the doctrine of Holy Scripture and the ordinance of God; but why is it so? Why is faith chosen rather than hope, or love, or patience?

It befits us to be modest in answering such a question, for God’s ways are not always to be understood; nor are we allowed to question them presumptuously. We would humbly reply that, as far as we can tell, faith has been chosen as the channel of grace because #emph[there is a natural fitness] in faith to be used as the receiver. Suppose I am about to give a poor man a gift: I put it into his hand — why? Well, it would hardly be fitting to put it into his ear, or to lay it on his foot; the hand seems made on purpose to receive. So, in the makeup of our minds, faith is created for the very purpose of being a receiver: it is the man’s hand, and there is a fitness in receiving grace by means of it.

Let me put this very plainly. Faith that receives Christ is as simple an act as when your child takes an apple from you, because you hold it out and promise to give him the apple if he comes for it. The believing and the receiving concern only an apple; but they make up exactly the same act as the faith that deals with eternal salvation. What the child’s hand is to the apple, your faith is to the perfect salvation of Christ. The child’s hand does not make the apple, nor improve the apple, nor deserve the apple; it only takes it; and faith is chosen by God to be the receiver of salvation, because it does not claim to create salvation, nor to help in it, but is content to receive it humbly. “Faith is the tongue that begs pardon, the hand which receives it, and the eye which sees it; but it is not the price which buys it.” Faith never makes herself her own plea; she rests her whole case on the blood of Christ. She makes a good servant for bringing the riches of the Lord Jesus to the soul, because she acknowledges where she got them, and confesses that grace alone entrusted her with them.

Again, faith is no doubt chosen because it #emph[gives all the glory to God]. It is by faith so that it may be by grace, and it is by grace so that there may be no boasting; for God cannot bear pride. “The proud He knows from afar,” and he has no wish to come nearer to them. He will not give salvation in a way that would suggest or foster pride. Paul says, “Not by works, so that no one can boast.” Now, faith shuts out all boasting. The hand that receives charity does not say, “I should be thanked for accepting the gift”; that would be absurd. When the hand carries bread to the mouth, it does not say to the body, “Thank me; for I feed you.” What the hand does is a very simple thing, though a very necessary one; and it never claims glory for itself for what it does. So God has chosen faith to receive the unspeakable gift of his grace, because it cannot take any credit to itself, but must adore the gracious God who is the giver of all good. Faith sets the crown on the right head, and therefore the Lord Jesus used to put the crown on the head of faith, saying, “Your faith has saved you; go in peace.”

Next, God chooses faith as the channel of salvation because #emph[it is a sure means of linking man with God]. When man trusts in God, there is a point of union between them, and that union guarantees blessing. Faith saves us because it makes us cling to God, and so brings us into connection with him. I have often used the following illustration, but I must repeat it, because I cannot think of a better one. I am told that years ago a boat capsized above Niagara Falls, and two men were being carried down by the current, when people on the shore managed to float a rope out to them, and both of them seized it. One of them held on tight and was pulled safely to the bank; but the other, seeing a great log come floating by, unwisely let go of the rope and clung to the log, for it was the bigger of the two things, and seemed better to cling to. Alas! The log, with the man on it, went right over into the vast abyss, because there was no union between the log and the shore. The size of the log was no help to the man who grasped it; it needed a connection with the shore to bring him to safety. So when a man trusts in his works, or in sacraments, or in anything of that kind, he will not be saved, because there is no link between him and Christ; but faith, though it may seem like a slender cord, is in the hand of the great God on the shore; infinite power pulls in the connecting line, and so draws the man out of destruction. Oh, the blessedness of faith, for it unites us to God!

Again, faith is chosen because it #emph[touches the mainsprings of action]. Even in ordinary things, faith of a sort lies at the root of everything. I wonder whether I would be wrong to say that we never do anything except through faith of some kind. If I walk across my study, it is because I believe my legs will carry me. A man eats because he believes in the necessity of food; he goes to work because he believes in the value of money; he accepts a check because he believes that the bank will honor it. Columbus discovered America because he believed that there was another continent beyond the ocean; and the Pilgrim Fathers colonized it because they believed that God would be with them on those rocky shores. Most great deeds have been born of faith; for good or for evil, faith works wonders through the man in whom it lives. Faith in its natural form is an all-prevailing force, which enters into every kind of human action. Perhaps the man who mocks faith in God is the very one who, in an evil form, has the most faith of all; indeed, he usually falls into a gullibility that would be ridiculous if it were not disgraceful. God gives salvation to faith because, by creating faith in us, he touches the real mainspring of our emotions and actions. He has, so to speak, taken possession of the battery, and now he can send the sacred current to every part of our nature. When we believe in Christ, and the heart has come into God’s possession, then we are saved from sin, and are moved toward repentance, holiness, zeal, prayer, consecration, and every other gracious thing. “What oil is to the wheels, what weights are to a clock, what wings are to a bird, what sails are to a ship, that faith is to all holy duties and services.” Have faith, and all the other graces will follow and keep on their course.

Again, faith #emph[has the power of working by love]\; it moves the affections toward God, and draws the heart after the best things. He who believes in God will, beyond all question, love God. Faith is an act of the understanding; but it also comes from the heart. “With the heart man believes and is justified”; and so God gives salvation to faith because it lives next door to the affections and is closely related to love; and love is the parent and the nurse of every holy feeling and act. Love for God is obedience; love for God is holiness. To love God and to love man is to be conformed to the image of Christ; and this is salvation.

Moreover, #emph[faith creates peace and joy]\; he who has it rests, and is calm, is glad and joyful; and this is a preparation for heaven. God gives all heavenly gifts to faith, for this reason among others: that faith produces in us the life and spirit which are to be shown forever in the higher and better world. Faith supplies us with armor for this life, and education for the life to come. It enables a man both to live and to die without fear; it prepares him both for action and for suffering; and so the Lord chooses it as a most fitting means of bringing grace to us, and in this way securing us for glory.

Certainly faith does for us what nothing else can do; it gives us joy and peace, and brings us into rest. Why do men try to gain salvation by other means? An old theologian says, “A foolish servant who is told to open a door sets his shoulder to it and pushes with all his might; but the door does not stir, and he cannot enter, whatever strength he uses. Another comes with a key, and easily unlocks the door, and enters very readily. Those who would be saved by works are pushing at heaven’s gate without result; but faith is the key which opens the gate at once.” Reader, will you not use that key? The Lord commands you to believe in his dear Son, so you may do so; and if you do, you will live. Is this not the promise of the gospel, “Whoever believes and is baptized will be saved”? Mark 16:16. What objection can you have to a way of salvation that commends itself to the mercy and the wisdom of our gracious God?
